-- title:
--   Unramified at tame j-finite places over X₀(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $M\ge 5$ and $p\nmid M$, and work inside the Laurent series field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $K_1$ be the intermediate field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-generated by the coefficientwise images of the elements of [`ModularCurve.x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ M p`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L142), that is of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by all quotients of $q$-expansions of two modular forms of one and the same weight for $\Gamma_1(M)\cap\Gamma_0(p)$ having integral $q$-expansions (the denominator expansion being nonzero), and let $K_2$ be the corresponding field built from $\Gamma_0(Mp)$ in place of $\Gamma_1(M)\cap\Gamma_0(p)$; assume $K_2\le K_1$. Let $J\in K_1$ be the element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular invariant, and let $x$ be a place of $K_1$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a proper valuation subring of $K_1$ containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a principal ideal ring) with $\operatorname{ord}_x J\ge 0$. Assume: if $\operatorname{ord}_x J>0$ then $p\not\equiv 1 \pmod 3$, and if moreover $p=3$ then some prime $\ell\mid M$ satisfies $\ell\not\equiv 1\pmod 3$; if $\operatorname{ord}_x(J-1728)>0$ then $p\not\equiv 1\pmod 4$, and if moreover $p=2$ then some prime $\ell\mid M$ satisfies $\ell\not\equiv 1\pmod 4$. Then the ramification index of $x$ along the inclusion $K_2\hookrightarrow K_1$, namely the least $n>0$ for which some nonzero $f\in K_2$ has $\operatorname{ord}_x f=n$, equals $1$.
--
--   This is the statement that, over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the covering of modular curves $X(\Gamma_1(M)\cap\Gamma_0(p))\to X_0(Mp)$ is unramified at every place where $j$ is finite, under congruence conditions on $p$ and $M$ ruling out elliptic points of $\Gamma_0(Mp)$ at $j=0$ and $j=1728$. It is the version over an algebraically closed base field, and is invoked by the corresponding statement for a general base.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_ramificationIndexAlong_inclusion_eq_one_of_ord_nonneg_laurentBaseChange_x1x0_gamma0_of_tame_algebraicClosure.lean

